 teach: {
  prompt: "A classmate says primary teeth are just small permanent teeth. Correct them with five structural differences and, for each, one clinical consequence.",
  model: "Thin enamel and dentine mean caries reaches the pulp quickly and bitewings are needed early. A large pulp with high horns, especially the mesial horn of 7.4 and 8.4, means Class II preparations expose the pulp easily and pulpotomy is a routine treatment. The cervical bulge with a constricted neck is what a stainless steel crown snaps over. Flared roots with no root trunk put the furcation at the neck, so infection appears as an interradicular radiolucency and roots fracture on extraction. Broad flat contacts hide proximal lesions and spread them wide, and occlusally sloping cervical enamel rods mean no gingival bevel in Class II boxes."
 },
