 apply: {
  vignette: "A 29-year-old reports that her jaw suddenly 'locked' three days ago. Maximum opening is 26 mm and on opening the mandible moves to the right and stays there. She has had clicking in the right joint for two years, now absent. Which movement is most likely lost?",
  options: ["Translation of the right condyle","Rotation of the left condyle","Translation of the left condyle","Rotation of the right condyle"],
  answer: "Translation of the right condyle",
  steps: [
   "Identify the ask: which component of mandibular movement, on which side, explains limited opening with deflection to the right.",
   "Facts that matter: opening limited to about the rotational range, deflection (not a returning deviation) toward the right, a right-sided click that has stopped.",
   "Eliminate rotation of either condyle: rotation in the lower compartment gives the first 20 to 25 mm, and she can open that far, so rotation is intact.",
   "Eliminate translation of the left condyle: the condyle that cannot slide forward holds its side back, so the jaw deflects toward the stuck side. A left block would deflect her to the left.",
   "The right condyle cannot translate because the displaced disc, which used to click back, now stays in front of it and blocks the slide in the upper compartment.",
   "Loss of right condylar translation explains the 26 mm opening, the deflection to the right and the vanished click, which is the pattern of disc displacement without reduction."
  ]
 },
 teach: {
  prompt: "Explain which determinants of occlusal morphology a dentist can change and which they cannot, and predict what happens to a new crown's cusp height if the patient has a large immediate side shift.",
  model: "The posterior determinants are the joints: condylar guidance, Bennett movement and intercondylar distance, all fixed by anatomy. The anterior determinant is anterior guidance, the incisor and canine overlap, which a dentist can change with restorations or orthodontics. A large immediate side shift moves the whole mandible bodily sideways early in the excursion, so cusps have less vertical clearance and must be shorter with wider fossae; if the crown is built with tall cusps on an articulator that ignored the shift, it will interfere on lateral excursion and need adjustment."
 },
