 questions: [
  ["During the first part of mouth opening, mandibular movement is mainly:","Rotation of the condyles in the lower joint compartment",["Translation of the condyles in the upper joint compartment","Rotation in the upper joint compartment","Translation in the lower joint compartment"],"Early opening is a hinge rotation between condyle and disc; translation along the eminence in the upper compartment takes over for wider opening. The compartments are not interchangeable."],
  ["The imaginary line through both condyles about which the mandible rotates during early opening is the:","Terminal hinge axis",["Bennett axis","Sagittal axis","Occlusal plane axis"],"The terminal hinge axis is the transverse rotation axis recorded by a facebow. The Bennett angle describes the orbiting condyle's path, not an axis."],
  ["During a left lateral excursion the right condyle:","Moves downward, forward and medially",["Rotates about a vertical axis and shifts laterally","Remains stationary","Moves upward and backward"],"The non-working (orbiting) condyle, here the right, travels down, forward and medially; the working (left) condyle rotates and shifts laterally. The angle of the orbiting path to the sagittal plane is the Bennett angle."],
  ["Bennett movement is best described as:","The bodily lateral shift of the mandible toward the working side",["The forward slide of both condyles down the eminence during protrusion","The rotation of the orbiting condyle about a vertical axis during excursion","The vertical drop of the working condyle during wide opening"],"Bennett movement (lateral translation) is the sideways bodily shift of the whole mandible; the orbiting condyle's path angle is the Bennett angle, a different quantity."],
  ["The most posterior-superior point of Posselt's envelope of motion represents:","Centric relation",["Maximum intercuspation","Postural rest position","Maximum protrusion"],"Centric relation is the terminal hinge position at the top of the posterior border; maximum intercuspation lies slightly anterior and superior to it on the envelope."],
  ["A patient's rest vertical dimension measures 68 mm and the freeway space is 3 mm. The vertical dimension of occlusion should be:","65 mm",["71 mm","68 mm","62 mm"],"VDO equals rest vertical dimension minus the freeway space: 68 minus 3 is 65 mm. Adding the freeway space would leave no interocclusal rest space."],
  ["A lateral excursion of the mandible to the left is produced mainly by the:","Right lateral pterygoid muscle",["Left lateral pterygoid muscle","Left masseter","Right temporalis"],"The lateral pterygoid pulls its condyle forward and medially, swinging the mandible to the opposite side; so the right muscle drives a left excursion. The temporalis retrudes and elevates."],
  ["On opening, a 35-year-old's mandible moves 4 mm to the left and then returns to the midline with an audible click. This is best described as:","Deviation, suggesting a reducing disc displacement on the left",["Deflection, suggesting a non-reducing disc displacement on the left","Deflection, suggesting a non-reducing disc displacement on the right","Normal symmetrical opening"],"A return to the midline is a deviation and the click marks the disc reducing onto the left condyle; a deflection stays to one side and indicates that condyle cannot translate."],
  ["Which determinant of occlusal morphology can the dentist alter?","Anterior guidance",["Condylar guidance","Intercondylar distance","Bennett angle"],"Anterior guidance is set by the incisor and canine overlap and can be changed with restorations or orthodontics; the joint-based posterior determinants are fixed anatomy."],
  ["A patient has a steep articular eminence. Compared with a patient with a flat eminence, the posterior cusps of a crown for 3.6 may be:","Taller, because the condyle drops more steeply in excursions",["Shorter, because the condyle drops more steeply","Taller, because the condyle does not move","Unchanged, because cusp height depends only on tooth size"],"Steeper condylar guidance separates the posterior teeth more quickly in protrusion and lateral movement, leaving room for taller cusps. Flat guidance demands shorter cusps."],
  ["A large immediate side shift during lateral excursion requires posterior restorations to have:","Shorter cusps and wider fossae",["Taller cusps and narrower fossae","Deeper central grooves only","No change in morphology"],"An immediate bodily shift moves the cusps sideways before any vertical separation occurs, so tall cusps would collide; shorter cusps with wider fossae clear the path."],
  ["On a semi-adjustable articulator, the condylar inclination is set using a:","Protrusive interocclusal record",["Facebow transfer record","Centric relation interocclusal record","Rest position record"],"The protrusive record captures how far the condyles drop as they move forward, which sets the condylar guidance; the facebow relates the maxilla to the hinge axis and the CR record mounts the mandibular cast."],
  ["The gothic arch (arrow-point) tracing used in complete denture construction records mandibular border movements in the horizontal plane; its apex represents:","Centric relation",["Maximum protrusion","Rest position","Maximum intercuspation"],"The apex of the arrow is the most retruded point from which the lateral paths diverge, which is centric relation. An edentulous patient has no maximum intercuspation to record."],
  ["A tooth close to the temporomandibular joint, such as 4.7, has its excursive pathways influenced mainly by:","Condylar guidance",["Anterior guidance","Incisal overjet","The curve of Wilson"],"The nearer a tooth is to the condyle, the more its movement follows the condylar path; anterior teeth are governed more by anterior guidance. Overjet is one element of anterior guidance."],
  ["During closing, the superior head of the lateral pterygoid contracts in order to:","Stabilise the disc on the condyle as it seats",["Pull the condyle forward to open the mouth","Protrude the mandible against resistance","Retrude the mandible into centric relation"],"The superior head is a closing-phase muscle that holds the disc forward against the condyle during power closure; the inferior head opens and protrudes. Retrusion is a temporalis action."]
 ]
